        "# Boosting\n",
        "\n",
        "## Boosting: The Big Idea {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "Sequentially fit models that correct previous errors\n",
        "\n",
        "### Difference vs bagging:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Bagging         | Boosting           |\n",
        "| --------------- | ------------------ |\n",
        "| Parallel        | Sequential         |\n",
        "| Reduce variance | Reduce bias        |\n",
        "| Robust to noise | Sensitive to noise |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why Boosting Works Well in Finance {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "- Many weak predictors\n",
        "- Small individual signal-to-noise ratio\n",
        "- Additive effects dominate\n",
        "\n",
        "Boosting:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Extracts small, consistent edges\n",
        "- Aggregates them into a strong signal\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## AdaBoost (Conceptual View) {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "- Often uses decision stumps\n",
        "- Reweights observations:\n",
        "    - Hard-to-predict points get more weight\n",
        "\n",
        "Finance analogy:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Difficult market conditions get more emphasis\n",
        "- Model focuses on rare but important patterns\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Gradient Boosting: The Core Idea {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "- Gradient Boosting builds an **additive model**\n",
        "- Models are trained **sequentially**\n",
        "- Each new tree:\n",
        "  - Corrects the **errors of the current model**\n",
        "\n",
        "General form:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_M(x) = \\sum_{m=1}^M \\nu \\cdot h_m(x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "- $h_m(x)$: weak learner (tree)\n",
        "- $\\nu$: learning rate (shrinkage)\n",
        "\n",
        "Key idea:\n",
        "> **Improve the model step by step, not all at once**\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Gradient Boosting for Regression {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Start with a constant model:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_0(x) = \\arg\\min_c \\sum_i (y_i - c)^2\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Compute **residuals**:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "r_i = y_i - F_{m-1}(x_i)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Fit a tree to the residuals:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_m(x) \\approx r\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "4. Update the model:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_m(x) = F_{m-1}(x) + \\nu h_m(x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Finance intuition:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Each tree explains **what the previous model missed**\n",
        "- Useful for returns, spreads, volatility\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Gradient Boosting for Classification {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "- Targets are **class labels**\n",
        "- Model predicts **log-odds / scores**\n",
        "- Uses a classification loss (e.g. logistic loss)\n",
        "\n",
        "At each step:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Compute the **negative gradient** of the loss\n",
        "- Fit a tree to this gradient\n",
        "- Update prediction scores\n",
        "\n",
        "Interpretation:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Trees correct **probability errors**, not labels directly\n",
        "- Final output → probabilities via sigmoid\n",
        "\n",
        "Finance examples:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Market direction (up/down)\n",
        "- Default vs non-default\n",
        "- Fraud detection\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## General Principles of Boosting {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "**Key hyperparameters:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- `n_estimators` → number of correction steps\n",
        "- `learning_rate` → step size\n",
        "- `max_depth` → interaction complexity\n",
        "- `subsample` → stochastic boosting\n",
        "\n",
        "**Trade-offs:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Small learning rate + many trees → stable, slow\n",
        "- Large learning rate → fast, overfitting\n",
        "\n",
        "**Finance rule of thumb:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Prefer **shallow trees**\n",
        "- Use **small learning rates**\n",
        "- Validate with **time-aware CV**\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.callout-important}\n",
        "> Gradient Boosting = controlled, incremental learning\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Boosting for Market Prediction {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "**Typical setup:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Shallow trees (max_depth = 2–4)\n",
        "- Small learning rate (0.01–0.1)\n",
        "- Many estimators\n",
        "\n",
        "**Advantages:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- High predictive accuracy\n",
        "- Captures subtle patterns\n",
        "\n",
        "**Risks:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Overfitting\n",
        "- Regime sensitivity\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Random Forest vs Boosting (Finance) {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "| Aspect               | Random Forest      | Boosting       |\n",
        "| -------------------- | ------------------ | -------------- |\n",
        "| Training             | Parallel           | Sequential     |\n",
        "| Main benefit         | Variance reduction | Bias reduction |\n",
        "| Noise robustness     | High               | Medium         |\n",
        "| Tuning effort        | Low                | High           |\n",
        "| Production stability | High               | Medium         |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Rule of Thumb for Finance Applications {.scrollable}\n",
        "\n",
        "- *Baseline / noisy signals* → Random Forest\n",
        "- *Alpha extraction* → Boosting\n",
        "- *Risk / credit scoring* → Random Forest\n",
        "- *Competitions / benchmarks* → Boosting\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python Example: Financial Regression {.scrollable}"
      ],
